window.ME_DATA = window.ME_DATA || {};
window.ME_DATA["y12-me"] = [
 {
  id:"l1", lesson:1, title:"Sheikh Zayed: Leadership Built on Values", unit:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach", art:"hero",
  dna:"Think of a leader (from any field, past or present) whom people trust. Write down ONE quality that earns that trust. Then ask yourself: is it possible to be an effective leader without being a moral one? Write one sentence with your view.",
  dnaQs:["Name ONE quality that makes a leader trustworthy.","Is it possible to be an effective leader without being a moral one? Give your view in one sentence."],
  dnaHint:"Strong answers name a specific quality (integrity, humility, consistency, listening) rather than a vague one like ‘good’. For the second question, take a position: some leaders achieve short-term results without morals, but trust, legitimacy and long-term loyalty usually depend on values. Distinguishing between ‘effective in the short term’ and ‘effective over time’ is a sophisticated move.",
  intentions:["Understand the key stages of Sheikh Zayed's life and leadership, from Al Ain to the founding of the UAE.","Analyse how values such as humility, wisdom, patience and closeness to people shaped his style of leadership.","Evaluate the claim that moral character is the foundation of effective and lasting leadership."],
  criteria:["I can outline the key stages of Sheikh Zayed's leadership with accurate dates.","I can analyse how specific values shaped his leadership decisions.","I can evaluate the relationship between moral character and effective leadership, considering more than one perspective."],
  concept:{key:"Identity", related:"Leadership, Values, Legitimacy", soi:"Leadership that is rooted in consistent moral values builds the trust and unity that allow a nation to form and endure.", context:"Identities and Relationships — exploring how the character of a leader shapes the identity of a nation.", uae:"Sheikh Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan (c.1918–2004), Ruler of Abu Dhabi from 1966 and first President of the UAE from 1971 to 2004; the UAE marked his centenary as the ‘Year of Zayed’ in 2018. Links to History (state formation) and English (analytical writing)."},
  words:[["Values-based leadership","A style of leadership in which decisions are guided by consistent moral principles rather than only by power or short-term gain."],["Legitimacy","The general acceptance by people that a leader's authority is rightful and deserves to be respected."],["Humility","A modest view of one's own importance, shown in openness to others and willingness to listen."],["Majlis","A traditional council or gathering where people meet their leader directly to share concerns and seek advice."],["Federation","A union of partly self-governing states under one central government, such as the seven emirates of the UAE."],["Vision","A clear, long-term idea of what a leader hopes to achieve for their people."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Create an annotated timeline of Sheikh Zayed's leadership with at least FIVE key moments (e.g. his role in Al Ain from 1946, becoming Ruler of Abu Dhabi in 1966, the founding of the UAE on 2 December 1971, his presidency until 2004).","Use at least two reliable sources (e.g. official UAE government portals, national archives, reputable encyclopaedias).","For each moment, note ONE value that his actions at that time demonstrated, with brief evidence.","Evaluate one source: how reliable is it, and is there any reason it might present a particular perspective?"], prompts:["My two sources","Five key moments with dates","The value shown at each moment, with evidence","An evaluation of one source's reliability and perspective"], success:"Strong timelines are accurate (born c.1918; Ruler's Representative in Al Ain from 1946; Ruler of Abu Dhabi from 1966; first President of the UAE 1971–2004; died 2004) and link each event to a specific value with evidence — for example, persuading the rulers of the emirates to unite shows patience and consultation; investing oil revenues in schools, housing and healthcare shows care for people. For the top band, evaluate a source critically: official sources are authoritative on dates and policies but naturally present the founder positively, so corroborating with an independent reference work strengthens the research."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"When oil revenues began to transform Abu Dhabi in the 1960s, a ruler could have used the new wealth mainly to build personal power. Instead, Sheikh Zayed is widely remembered for directing it towards schools, hospitals, housing and infrastructure, and for continuing to meet ordinary citizens in the majlis, listening to their concerns in person.", steps:["Write an analytical paragraph (point, evidence, explanation, evaluation, link) explaining what this extract reveals about values-based leadership.","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank (e.g. legitimacy, humility, majlis, vision) accurately.","Include an evaluative sentence: why might values-based leadership be more durable than leadership based on power alone?"], model:"The extract suggests that Sheikh Zayed's leadership was grounded in values rather than in the accumulation of personal power. By directing new oil wealth towards schools, hospitals and housing, he showed a long-term vision in which the nation's resources served its people, and by continuing to meet citizens in the majlis he demonstrated humility and accessibility. These choices strengthened his legitimacy: people accepted his authority not only because he held office but because they saw it being used for their benefit. Values-based leadership is arguably more durable than leadership based on power alone, because trust earned through consistent conduct survives difficult moments, whereas authority based only on control tends to weaken when that control is challenged.", rubric:[["1–2","Descriptive rather than analytical; little or no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","Some analysis but undeveloped; 1–2 key terms used, possibly inaccurately."],["5–6","Clear analytical paragraph; 3 key terms used accurately; evaluation attempted."],["7–8","Sophisticated, well-structured analysis; 4+ key terms used precisely; evaluation is convincing and considers why values create lasting legitimacy."]], starters:["The extract suggests that Sheikh Zayed's leadership was grounded in ___.","By directing wealth towards ___, he demonstrated ___.","This strengthened his legitimacy because ___.","Values-based leadership is arguably more durable because ___, whereas ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Leadership Values Profile", steps:["In groups of 3–4, identify FOUR core values that characterised Sheikh Zayed's leadership.","For each value, find one well-documented example from his life and explain how it influenced a decision or outcome.","Compare: choose ONE of these values and assess how a leader today (in any field — school, business, community) could apply it, and what obstacles they might face.","Design a one-page ‘Leadership Values Profile’ (infographic or poster) presenting your findings.","Present in a 4-minute pitch and answer one challenge question from the class. Paraphrase his views accurately rather than inventing quotations."], rubric:[["1–2","Values are listed without evidence; no application to the present."],["3–4","Values supported by general examples; application is vague."],["5–6","Values supported by accurate, specific examples; realistic application with an obstacle identified."],["7–8","Insightful, well-evidenced profile; application to the present is critical and nuanced, weighing obstacles convincingly."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Values-Based or Power-Based?", prompt:"Sort each leadership action: does it reflect values-based leadership, or leadership based mainly on power and self-interest?", bins:["Values-based leadership","Power-based / self-interested"], items:[["Meeting ordinary citizens regularly to hear their concerns",0],["Investing national wealth in education and healthcare for all",0],["Persuading partners patiently to unite rather than forcing them",0],["Using public funds mainly to enrich a small circle of allies",1],["Refusing to listen to advisers who disagree",1],["Making decisions with the next generation in mind",0],["Silencing complaints instead of addressing their causes",1],["Taking credit for others' work to look stronger",1]]},
  quiz:[["In which year did Sheikh Zayed become Ruler of Abu Dhabi?",["1946","1966","1971"],1],["The United Arab Emirates was founded on…",["2 December 1971","6 August 1966","2 November 2004"],0],["What does 'legitimacy' mean in the context of leadership?",["The amount of money a leader controls","The size of a leader's army","The general acceptance that a leader's authority is rightful"],2],["What was the purpose of the traditional majlis?",["A place where citizens could meet their leader directly to share concerns","A military training ground","A marketplace for trade"],0],["Which choice best illustrates Sheikh Zayed's long-term vision?",["Keeping oil revenues secret","Investing oil revenues in schools, hospitals and housing","Limiting education to a few families"],1],["Why did the UAE name 2018 the ‘Year of Zayed’?",["It was the year the UAE was founded","It was the year oil was discovered","It marked the centenary of his birth"],2],["Which argument best supports the claim that values-based leadership is more durable?",["Trust earned through consistent conduct survives difficult moments","Leaders with values never face criticism","Power always lasts longer than trust"],0],["How long did Sheikh Zayed serve as President of the UAE?",["About 10 years","From 1971 until his death in 2004","Only during 1971"],1]],
  exit:"In one sentence, identify the ONE value you think was most central to Sheikh Zayed's leadership, and justify your choice with evidence.",
  copy:{facts:["Sheikh Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan (c.1918–2004) became Ruler of Abu Dhabi in 1966 and was the first President of the UAE from 1971 until his death in 2004.","His leadership is remembered for values such as humility, patience, consultation and care for people — shown in investing oil wealth in education, health and housing.","Values-based leadership builds legitimacy: people accept authority they see being used for their benefit. The UAE marked his centenary as the ‘Year of Zayed’ in 2018."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by examining tolerance and coexistence in Zayed's vision."
 },
 {
  id:"l2", lesson:2, title:"Tolerance and Coexistence in Zayed's Vision", unit:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach", art:"tolerance",
  dna:"Recap: name ONE value that shaped Sheikh Zayed's leadership. Now consider: the UAE is home to people of around 200 nationalities. Is ‘tolerance’ enough for a diverse society to thrive, or does it need something more? Write one sentence explaining your view.",
  dnaQs:["Name ONE value that shaped Sheikh Zayed's leadership (from last lesson).","Is ‘tolerance’ enough for a diverse society to thrive, or does it need something more?"],
  dnaHint:"For the recap: humility, patience, consultation or care for people are all good answers. For the second question, a strong response distinguishes between tolerance (accepting differences and not discriminating) and coexistence or fraternity (actively living and working together with mutual respect). Many thinkers argue that tolerance is a minimum, while genuine coexistence requires positive engagement.",
  intentions:["Understand how tolerance and coexistence formed part of Sheikh Zayed's moral approach and the UAE's identity.","Distinguish between tolerance, acceptance and active coexistence.","Evaluate how the UAE's institutions and initiatives continue this vision today, and the limits of tolerance."],
  criteria:["I can explain the role of tolerance and coexistence in Zayed's vision with accurate examples.","I can distinguish clearly between tolerance, acceptance and coexistence.","I can evaluate how far modern initiatives reflect this vision, including the question of whether tolerance has limits."],
  concept:{key:"Global interactions", related:"Diversity, Respect, Coexistence", soi:"Societies that move beyond simple tolerance towards active coexistence create the conditions for shared prosperity and peace.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring how diverse communities live and work together.", uae:"The UAE established a Ministry of Tolerance in 2016 and declared 2019 the ‘Year of Tolerance’; the Document on Human Fraternity was signed in Abu Dhabi in 2019. Links to Islamic Education (respect for others) and TOK (moral relativism)."},
  words:[["Tolerance","The willingness to accept beliefs, practices or people that differ from one's own, without discrimination."],["Coexistence","Living together peacefully with mutual respect, despite differences in belief, culture or background."],["Pluralism","A society in which people of many different backgrounds and beliefs live together and participate as equals."],["Discrimination","Unjust treatment of people because of characteristics such as religion, ethnicity or nationality."],["Fraternity","A sense of brotherhood and shared humanity between people of different backgrounds."],["Social cohesion","The bonds of trust and solidarity that hold a diverse society together."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE UAE initiative that reflects tolerance and coexistence (e.g. the Ministry of Tolerance established in 2016, the Year of Tolerance 2019, the Document on Human Fraternity signed in Abu Dhabi in 2019, or the Abrahamic Family House).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: what it is, when it began, and what it aims to achieve.","Explain how it connects to the values Sheikh Zayed promoted.","Identify one challenge or criticism such initiatives might face, and how it could be addressed."], prompts:["The initiative I chose and my two sources","What it is, when it began and what it aims to achieve","How it connects to Zayed's values","One challenge or criticism, and how it could be addressed"], success:"Strong answers give accurate details (for example, the Document on Human Fraternity was signed in Abu Dhabi in February 2019 by Pope Francis and the Grand Imam of Al-Azhar) and make an explicit link to Zayed's vision of a society where people of different nationalities and faiths live in mutual respect. For the top band, engage critically: a common challenge is the gap between symbolic initiatives and everyday experience, so the most convincing answers suggest how a policy is felt in daily life — in workplaces, schools and neighbourhoods."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A philosopher once argued that a tolerant society faces a paradox: if it tolerates everything, including those who preach hatred and intolerance, it may eventually be destroyed by them. Some therefore argue that tolerance must have limits.", steps:["Write an argumentative paragraph responding to the question: ‘Should tolerance have limits?’","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank (e.g. tolerance, coexistence, discrimination, social cohesion) accurately.","Refer to the UAE context (e.g. its anti-discrimination legislation or its vision of coexistence) and reach a justified conclusion."], model:"Tolerance is essential to a pluralistic society, yet it cannot be unlimited without undermining itself. If a society tolerated hate speech and discrimination, the very people who most depend on tolerance — minorities and newcomers — would lose their security, and social cohesion would break down. For this reason, many states, including the UAE through its anti-discrimination legislation, protect freedom of belief while prohibiting incitement to hatred. This reflects the logic of Zayed's vision: coexistence was not indifference to all behaviour, but mutual respect between people. I therefore conclude that tolerance should have limits, but those limits must be narrow and principled, targeting harm to others rather than mere difference of opinion.", rubric:[["1–2","Little argument; limited or no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","A basic position with limited justification; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","A clear, justified argument; 3 key terms used accurately; some reference to the UAE context."],["7–8","A sophisticated, balanced argument; 4+ key terms used precisely; UAE context integrated; conclusion is nuanced and principled."]], starters:["Tolerance is essential to a pluralistic society, yet ___.","If a society tolerated ___, then ___.","This reflects Zayed's vision because ___.","I therefore conclude that tolerance should / should not have limits, because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Coexistence Audit", steps:["In groups of 3–4, design a short anonymous survey (5–6 questions) to explore how students in your year group experience coexistence in school life.","Ensure questions are respectful, neutral and avoid identifying individuals.","Collect responses from at least 15 peers (with teacher approval) and summarise the findings.","Identify ONE strength and ONE area for development in your school's culture of coexistence.","Propose a practical initiative inspired by Zayed's values and present it in a 4-minute pitch, using Word Bank vocabulary."], rubric:[["1–2","Survey poorly designed or biased; findings not analysed."],["3–4","Basic survey; findings described but not interpreted."],["5–6","Well-designed survey; findings analysed; realistic initiative proposed."],["7–8","Ethical, well-designed survey; insightful analysis of findings; initiative is practical, evidence-based and clearly linked to Zayed's vision."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Tolerance, Coexistence or Neither?", prompt:"Sort each example: does it show basic tolerance (accepting difference), active coexistence (engaging together), or neither?", bins:["Basic tolerance","Active coexistence","Neither (discrimination)"], items:[["Not objecting when a neighbour celebrates a different religious festival",0],["Inviting colleagues of different faiths to share an iftar",1],["Refusing to hire someone because of their nationality",2],["Accepting that classmates hold different opinions without mocking them",0],["Organising a joint community project with people from many cultures",1],["Spreading jokes that stereotype a particular ethnic group",2],["Learning about a classmate's culture and sharing your own",1],["Excluding someone from a group because of their accent",2]]},
  quiz:[["In which year did the UAE establish a Ministry of Tolerance?",["2016","2004","2019"],0],["Which year did the UAE declare the ‘Year of Tolerance’?",["2018","2021","2019"],2],["What is the difference between tolerance and coexistence?",["They mean exactly the same","Tolerance accepts difference; coexistence involves actively living together with mutual respect","Coexistence means everyone must think alike"],1],["What does 'pluralism' describe?",["A society where people of many backgrounds participate as equals","A society with one official culture only","A type of economic system"],0],["The Document on Human Fraternity (2019) was signed in…",["Cairo","Rome","Abu Dhabi"],2],["What is the ‘paradox of tolerance’?",["Tolerance always makes societies weaker","Unlimited tolerance of intolerance may allow intolerance to destroy a tolerant society","Tolerance is impossible to define"],1],["Which is the most principled limit on tolerance?",["Banning anyone with different opinions","Prohibiting incitement to hatred while protecting freedom of belief","Tolerating only people from your own country"],1],["How does Zayed's vision relate to the UAE's diversity today?",["It encouraged mutual respect among people of many nationalities and faiths","It aimed to keep communities completely separate","It had no connection to diversity"],0]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain the difference between tolerating someone and coexisting with them.",
  copy:{facts:["Tolerance means accepting difference without discrimination; coexistence goes further, meaning people actively live and work together with mutual respect.","Zayed's vision of coexistence continues in UAE initiatives: the Ministry of Tolerance (2016), the Year of Tolerance (2019) and the Document on Human Fraternity signed in Abu Dhabi (2019).","Tolerance arguably needs principled limits: protecting freedom of belief while prohibiting incitement to hatred keeps a diverse society cohesive."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by exploring generosity, giving and humanitarian work in Zayed's approach."
 },
 {
  id:"l3", lesson:3, title:"Generosity, Giving and Humanitarian Work", unit:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach", art:"globe",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, explain the difference between tolerance and coexistence. Now consider: if a wealthy country gives aid to another nation, is that generosity, or is it in its own interest? Can it be both? Write one sentence.",
  dnaQs:["What is the difference between tolerance and coexistence (from last lesson)?","Is a wealthy country's aid generosity, self-interest, or both?"],
  dnaHint:"For the recap: tolerance accepts difference, while coexistence means actively living together with mutual respect. For the second question, a nuanced answer recognises that motives can be mixed: aid can relieve suffering and also build friendships and stability. Many ethical traditions, including Islamic teaching on sincere intention (niyyah), suggest the purity of the motive matters, but the benefit to people in need matters too.",
  intentions:["Understand the place of generosity and humanitarian giving in Sheikh Zayed's moral approach and in Islamic values.","Analyse how this legacy continues through UAE humanitarian institutions and Zayed Humanitarian Day.","Evaluate what makes giving ethical, effective and respectful of the dignity of those who receive it."],
  criteria:["I can explain the role of generosity in Zayed's approach and its roots in Islamic values.","I can analyse at least one UAE humanitarian institution or initiative accurately.","I can evaluate what makes humanitarian giving ethical and effective, considering different perspectives."],
  concept:{key:"Global interactions", related:"Responsibility, Compassion, Dignity", soi:"Generosity that respects the dignity of recipients and addresses real needs reflects a moral responsibility that extends beyond national borders.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring responsibility for those in need, locally and globally.", uae:"Zayed Humanitarian Day is marked on 19 Ramadan, the anniversary of his death; the UAE's humanitarian work continues through institutions such as the Emirates Red Crescent and the Abu Dhabi Fund for Development. Links to Islamic Education (zakat and sadaqah) and Economics (development aid)."},
  words:[["Humanitarianism","Active concern for human welfare, especially through relieving suffering in crises."],["Philanthropy","The desire to promote the welfare of others, often through generous donations of money or resources."],["Zakat","The obligatory annual giving of a portion of wealth in Islam, one of the five pillars."],["Sadaqah","Voluntary charitable giving in Islam, done out of compassion and sincere intention."],["Sustainable development","Development that meets present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs."],["Dignity of the recipient","The principle that aid should be given in a way that respects the worth, agency and privacy of those who receive it."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE UAE humanitarian institution or project (e.g. the Emirates Red Crescent, the Abu Dhabi Fund for Development, the Zayed Charitable and Humanitarian Foundation, or a well-documented UAE relief effort).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: its purpose, the type of aid it provides, and one specific example of its work.","Classify the example: is it emergency relief, or long-term development? Explain the difference.","Link it to Zayed's legacy and to Zayed Humanitarian Day (19 Ramadan)."], prompts:["The institution or project I chose and my two sources","Its purpose and the type of aid it provides","One specific example of its work","Emergency relief or long-term development? Why?","How it links to Zayed's legacy"], success:"Strong answers give a specific, verifiable example (a country, a project type or a year) rather than a vague description. The distinction between emergency relief (food, shelter and medical care in a crisis) and long-term development (schools, hospitals, water systems, infrastructure) should be explained clearly. For the top band, evaluate why both matter: emergency relief saves lives immediately, while development reduces future vulnerability — reflecting Zayed's view that giving should improve people's lives in a lasting way."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"After a natural disaster, a charity posts photographs online of aid recipients receiving food parcels, showing their faces clearly. The posts raise large amounts of money for further aid, but some critics argue they compromise the dignity of the people pictured.", steps:["Write an evaluative paragraph addressing the question: ‘Does the good achieved by the charity justify the way it publicises its work?’","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank (e.g. humanitarianism, dignity of the recipient, sadaqah, philanthropy) accurately.","Draw on the Islamic principle that the best charity is often given discreetly, and reach a balanced, justified conclusion."], model:"The charity's publicity clearly achieves a humanitarian good, since the funds raised allow further aid to reach people in crisis. However, the principle of the dignity of the recipient suggests that people in need should not become a spectacle in exchange for support. Islamic teaching on sadaqah values discreet giving, warning that charity should not humiliate the person who receives it, and this reflects a wider ethical concern that philanthropy must respect people's privacy and agency. A balanced approach would allow the charity to publicise its work while protecting recipients, for example by obtaining consent, avoiding identifiable faces or telling stories through the voices of those helped. I therefore conclude that the good achieved does not fully justify the method, because effective giving and respectful giving need not be in conflict.", rubric:[["1–2","Little evaluation; limited or no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","A basic judgement with limited justification; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","A clear evaluation weighing both sides; 3 key terms used accurately; reference to Islamic principles."],["7–8","A sophisticated, balanced evaluation; 4+ key terms used precisely; principles applied thoughtfully; conclusion offers a practical resolution."]], starters:["The charity's publicity clearly achieves ___, since ___.","However, the principle of the dignity of the recipient suggests ___.","Islamic teaching on sadaqah values ___, which ___.","A balanced approach would ___, so I conclude that ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Ethical Giving Campaign", steps:["In groups of 3–4, design a school campaign for Zayed Humanitarian Day (19 Ramadan) that supports a real, reputable UAE charity.","Decide whether your campaign supports emergency relief or long-term development, and justify your choice.","Write a short ‘Ethical Giving Charter’ (4–5 principles) your campaign will follow, e.g. protecting recipients' dignity, transparency about where money goes.","Design campaign materials (poster, short script or social media plan) that follow your charter.","Present in a 4-minute pitch, explaining how your campaign reflects Zayed's legacy of giving."], rubric:[["1–2","Campaign is vague; no ethical principles identified."],["3–4","Basic campaign with general principles; limited justification."],["5–6","Clear campaign with a justified focus; ethical charter is relevant and applied in materials."],["7–8","Realistic, well-justified campaign; ethical charter is thoughtful and consistently applied; strong, specific link to Zayed's legacy."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Relief or Development?", prompt:"Sort each humanitarian action: is it emergency relief (immediate survival) or long-term development (lasting improvement)?", bins:["Emergency relief","Long-term development"], items:[["Sending food parcels and tents after an earthquake",0],["Building a school in a rural community",1],["Flying in medical teams to treat the injured after a flood",0],["Funding a water purification system for a town",1],["Providing temporary shelters for displaced families",0],["Training local nurses and doctors",1],["Distributing blankets during a sudden cold-weather crisis",0],["Rebuilding infrastructure such as dams and roads",1]]},
  quiz:[["When is Zayed Humanitarian Day marked?",["19 Ramadan","2 December","1 Muharram"],0],["Why was 19 Ramadan chosen?",["It is the anniversary of the UAE's founding","It is the anniversary of Sheikh Zayed's death","It is the first day of Eid"],1],["What is the difference between zakat and sadaqah?",["There is no difference","Sadaqah is obligatory; zakat is voluntary","Zakat is obligatory annual giving; sadaqah is voluntary charity"],2],["What does 'dignity of the recipient' mean?",["Aid should be given in a way that respects the worth and privacy of those who receive it","Recipients must thank donors publicly","Only wealthy people deserve aid"],0],["Building a hospital in a developing region is an example of…",["Emergency relief","Long-term development","Neither"],1],["Which is a strong argument for long-term development aid?",["It is always cheaper than emergency aid","It means emergencies will never happen","It reduces future vulnerability and dependence"],2],["Can a country's aid be both generous and in its own interest?",["Yes — motives can be mixed, but the benefit to people in need still matters","No — any self-interest cancels generosity","No — aid is always purely selfish"],0],["Which UAE institution is primarily associated with humanitarian relief?",["The Emirates Red Crescent","The Federal National Council","The Ministry of Tolerance"],0]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain ONE principle that makes giving ethical, not just generous.",
  copy:{facts:["Generosity was central to Sheikh Zayed's moral approach and is rooted in the Islamic values of zakat (obligatory giving) and sadaqah (voluntary charity).","Zayed Humanitarian Day, marked on 19 Ramadan, the anniversary of his death, continues his legacy through UAE institutions such as the Emirates Red Crescent.","Ethical giving balances emergency relief with long-term development and always protects the dignity of the recipient."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by exploring justice, consultation (shura) and unity in Zayed's approach."
 },
 {
  id:"l4", lesson:4, title:"Justice, Consultation (Shura) and Unity", unit:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach", art:"fair",
  dna:"Recap: name ONE principle of ethical giving from last lesson. Now consider: in 1971, the rulers of several separate emirates agreed to form one federation. Why might it be difficult for independent rulers to agree to unite? Write down TWO possible obstacles.",
  dnaQs:["Name ONE principle of ethical giving (from last lesson).","Why might it be difficult for independent rulers to agree to unite? Give TWO obstacles."],
  dnaHint:"For the recap: respecting the dignity of the recipient, transparency, or balancing relief with long-term development. Obstacles to union could include fear of losing independence, differences in wealth and population between emirates, disagreements over how power would be shared, and historic rivalries. Good answers recognise that unity required trust and fair compromise.",
  intentions:["Understand the principles of justice and consultation (shura) and their roots in Islamic and Emirati tradition.","Analyse how consultation and fairness helped Sheikh Zayed build unity among the emirates.","Evaluate the strengths and challenges of consultative decision-making in a modern nation and in everyday life."],
  criteria:["I can explain the principles of justice and shura with accurate references to tradition.","I can analyse how consultation and fairness contributed to the formation and unity of the UAE.","I can evaluate the strengths and limitations of consultative decision-making in different contexts."],
  concept:{key:"Systems", related:"Justice, Consultation, Unity", soi:"Decision-making that is just and consultative builds the trust needed to unite diverse groups into a stable whole.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how fair processes create durable unity.", uae:"The principle of shura (consultation) is referenced in the Qur'an (Surat Ash-Shura); the UAE federation was formed on 2 December 1971 and the Federal National Council was established in 1972 as a consultative body. Links to Global Politics and History."},
  words:[["Shura","The Islamic principle of consultation, in which decisions are made after seeking the views of others."],["Justice","Fair treatment of all people, giving each person what they are rightfully due."],["Consensus","General agreement reached by a group after discussion, rather than by imposing one view."],["Unity","The state of being joined together as a whole, while respecting the identities of the parts."],["Compromise","An agreement in which each side gives up something to reach a fair settlement."],["Equity","Fairness that takes into account different needs and circumstances, rather than treating everyone identically."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion A — Knowing and Understanding", steps:["Research the formation of the UAE federation in 1971 and the role of consultation in it, using at least two reliable sources.","Record: which emirates formed the union on 2 December 1971, and which emirate joined shortly afterwards in 1972.","Identify ONE example of compromise or consultation that helped make the union possible (e.g. negotiations between rulers, agreements on how the federation would be governed).","Research the role of the Federal National Council (established 1972) and explain how it reflects the principle of shura."], prompts:["My two sources","The emirates that formed the union in 1971, and the one that joined in 1972","One example of compromise or consultation","How the Federal National Council reflects shura"], success:"Accurate answers note that six emirates formed the union on 2 December 1971 (Abu Dhabi, Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, Umm Al Quwain and Fujairah), with Ras Al Khaimah joining in early 1972. Strong answers explain that the union required rulers to negotiate how power and resources would be shared, and that Sheikh Zayed is remembered for patient persuasion rather than force. For the top band, explain the Federal National Council as a consultative body that reviews legislation and raises citizens' concerns, linking it explicitly to the tradition of the majlis and the principle of shura."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A student council must decide how to spend a limited budget. One member suggests that the council president should simply decide, because consulting everyone will take too long. Another argues that every year group must be consulted first, even if the decision is delayed.", steps:["Write an evaluative paragraph on the question: ‘Is consultation always worth the time it takes?’","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank (e.g. shura, consensus, justice, equity) accurately.","Draw on the example of the UAE's formation and reach a justified conclusion that recognises when quick decisions may be necessary."], model:"Consultation is often worth the time it takes, because decisions made through shura carry greater legitimacy and are more likely to be accepted. The formation of the UAE illustrates this: rather than imposing a union, the rulers negotiated and compromised until a consensus was reached, and that patient process helped create a federation that has endured for over five decades. In the student council, consulting every year group would promote equity, ensuring that the needs of younger and older students are both considered, rather than reflecting only the president's view. However, consultation is not always appropriate: in an emergency, a delay could cause harm, so a leader may need to act quickly and explain the decision afterwards. I therefore conclude that consultation should be the default for decisions that affect a whole community, while rapid decisions should be reserved for genuine urgency and remain accountable to those affected.", rubric:[["1–2","Little evaluation; limited or no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","A basic position with limited justification; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","A clear evaluation with reference to the UAE example; 3 key terms used accurately."],["7–8","A sophisticated, balanced evaluation; 4+ key terms used precisely; UAE example integrated; conclusion distinguishes clearly between contexts."]], starters:["Consultation is often worth the time because ___.","The formation of the UAE illustrates this, since ___.","In the student council, consulting every year group would ___.","However, consultation may not be appropriate when ___, so I conclude ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Shura Simulation", steps:["In groups of 5–6, each member takes the role of a representative of a fictional community with different needs, resources and priorities.","Your groups must agree on a shared plan for a limited resource (e.g. a budget for a new community facility) through consultation.","Record the process: what each representative wanted, what compromises were made, and how consensus was (or was not) reached.","Compare your process with the principles of shura and the formation of the UAE.","Present a 4-minute reflection: what made consultation succeed or struggle, and what did you learn about justice and unity?"], rubric:[["1–2","Little genuine consultation; reflection is superficial."],["3–4","Some consultation; reflection describes the process without analysis."],["5–6","Genuine consultation with recorded compromises; clear comparison with shura and the UAE's formation."],["7–8","Skilful consultation and principled compromise; insightful, critical reflection linking the experience to justice, equity and unity."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Shura or Not?", prompt:"Sort each decision-making approach: does it reflect the principle of shura (consultation), or not?", bins:["Reflects shura","Does not reflect shura"], items:[["A leader seeks advice from experienced advisers before deciding",0],["A council discusses a proposal and reaches consensus",0],["A ruler makes a major decision without hearing anyone affected",1],["Citizens raise concerns through a representative body",0],["A group leader ignores members' ideas and decides alone",1],["Representatives negotiate and compromise to reach agreement",0],["Silencing a minority view to save time",1],["Holding a majlis where people can share their views openly",0]]},
  quiz:[["What does 'shura' mean?",["The principle of consultation in decision-making","A type of tax","A traditional dance"],0],["How many emirates formed the UAE on 2 December 1971?",["Seven","Six","Five"],1],["Which emirate joined the federation in early 1972?",["Sharjah","Fujairah","Ras Al Khaimah"],2],["What is the Federal National Council?",["A sports organisation","A consultative body established in 1972","A private company"],1],["What is the difference between equality and equity?",["Equity takes different needs into account; equality treats everyone identically","They are exactly the same","Equality is always unfair"],0],["What does 'consensus' mean?",["One person's decision","A vote won by a single person","General agreement reached by a group after discussion"],2],["Why did consultation help the UAE federation endure?",["It allowed one emirate to control the others","Decisions reached through compromise carried greater legitimacy and trust","It meant no decisions were ever made"],1],["When might a quick decision without full consultation be justified?",["In a genuine emergency where delay could cause harm","Whenever the leader is busy","Never under any circumstances"],0]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain how consultation (shura) helped create unity in the UAE.",
  copy:{facts:["Shura (consultation) is an Islamic principle, referenced in the Qur'an, that decisions should be made after seeking the views of others.","Six emirates formed the UAE on 2 December 1971, with Ras Al Khaimah joining in 1972; the union depended on negotiation, compromise and trust.","The Federal National Council (1972) and the tradition of the majlis reflect consultation in UAE governance; just and consultative processes build lasting unity."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by examining Sheikh Zayed's stewardship of the environment."
 },
 {
  id:"l5", lesson:5, title:"Stewardship of the Environment", unit:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach", art:"nature",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, explain the principle of shura. Now consider: the UAE has one of the harshest desert climates on Earth. What moral responsibility, if any, do we have to protect the environment for people who are not yet born? Write one sentence.",
  dnaQs:["In one sentence, explain the principle of shura (from last lesson).","What moral responsibility do we have to protect the environment for people not yet born?"],
  dnaHint:"For the recap: shura is the principle of consultation — making decisions after seeking the views of others. For the second question, a strong answer uses the idea of intergenerational responsibility: future generations cannot speak for themselves, so present generations act as trustees of the environment. Many Islamic scholars describe humans as khalifah (stewards or custodians) of the Earth.",
  intentions:["Understand Sheikh Zayed's commitment to environmental stewardship, including afforestation and wildlife conservation.","Analyse the moral foundations of environmental stewardship, including the Islamic concept of khalifah.","Evaluate the ethical tensions between economic development and environmental protection, then and now."],
  criteria:["I can explain specific examples of Zayed's environmental work accurately.","I can analyse the moral and religious foundations of environmental stewardship.","I can evaluate the tension between development and conservation, proposing a justified position."],
  concept:{key:"Sustainability", related:"Stewardship, Responsibility, Future generations", soi:"Treating the natural environment as a trust to be protected for future generations is a moral responsibility, not only a practical choice.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring humanity's responsibility for the natural world.", uae:"Sheikh Zayed championed afforestation in the desert, the Sir Bani Yas Island wildlife reserve, the protection of species such as the Arabian oryx, and the responsible practice of falconry; the UAE hosted COP28 in 2023. Links to Biology/ESS and Islamic Education (khalifah)."},
  words:[["Stewardship","The responsible care and management of something entrusted to one's protection, such as the environment."],["Khalifah","In Islamic teaching, the role of humans as custodians or trustees of the Earth."],["Afforestation","Planting trees in an area where there was previously no forest, such as desert land."],["Conservation","The protection and preservation of the natural environment, wildlife and resources."],["Intergenerational justice","The principle that present generations have duties to protect the interests of future generations."],["Biodiversity","The variety of plant and animal life in a particular habitat or in the world as a whole."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE environmental initiative associated with Sheikh Zayed (e.g. desert afforestation, the Sir Bani Yas Island wildlife reserve, the protection of the Arabian oryx, or his support for sustainable falconry and the protection of houbara bustards).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: what the initiative involved, what environmental challenge it addressed, and its outcome.","Identify which moral value it reflects (e.g. stewardship, intergenerational justice).","Connect it to ONE current UAE environmental effort (e.g. mangrove planting, COP28 in 2023, the UAE Net Zero 2050 initiative)."], prompts:["The initiative I chose and my two sources","What it involved and the challenge it addressed","Its outcome","The moral value it reflects","One current UAE effort that continues it"], success:"Strong answers are specific: for example, Sir Bani Yas Island was developed into a wildlife reserve where species such as the Arabian oryx were bred and protected, and afforestation transformed areas of desert through large-scale tree planting. Avoid inventing statistics — use figures only if a reliable source gives them. For the top band, explain the moral reasoning (Zayed's view that the environment is a trust for future generations, in line with the concept of khalifah) and make a clear, accurate link to a current initiative."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A coastal city plans to clear an area of mangrove forest to build a new luxury resort. The project would create hundreds of jobs and boost tourism, but the mangroves protect the coastline, store carbon and provide a habitat for many species.", steps:["Write an evaluative paragraph on the question: ‘Should economic development ever take priority over environmental protection?’","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank (e.g. stewardship, intergenerational justice, conservation, biodiversity) accurately.","Refer to Zayed's approach to the environment and reach a justified conclusion, considering whether a compromise is possible."], model:"Economic development brings real benefits, such as jobs and tourism, but it should not automatically take priority over environmental protection. Mangroves provide services that are difficult to replace: they protect the coastline, store carbon and sustain biodiversity, so clearing them would shift costs onto future generations who have no voice in the decision, which conflicts with the principle of intergenerational justice. Sheikh Zayed's approach suggests a different path: he pursued rapid development while treating the environment as a trust, reflecting the Islamic idea of humans as khalifah. A compromise could involve building the resort on less sensitive land or designing it around the mangroves as an eco-tourism attraction. I therefore conclude that development is legitimate, but stewardship requires that irreplaceable ecosystems be protected first, with development adapted to fit within environmental limits.", rubric:[["1–2","Little evaluation; limited or no key vocabulary used."],["3–4","A basic position with limited justification; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","A clear evaluation weighing both sides; 3 key terms used accurately; reference to Zayed's approach."],["7–8","A sophisticated, balanced evaluation; 4+ key terms used precisely; Zayed's approach integrated; realistic compromise proposed and justified."]], starters:["Economic development brings benefits such as ___, but ___.","Clearing the mangroves would ___, which conflicts with ___.","Sheikh Zayed's approach suggests ___.","A compromise could involve ___, so I conclude that ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Stewardship Action Proposal", steps:["In groups of 3–4, identify ONE environmental issue affecting your school or local community in the UAE (e.g. single-use plastics, water consumption, energy use, food waste).","Gather simple evidence of the issue (e.g. a short audit or observation over a week), with teacher approval.","Design a realistic action proposal inspired by Zayed's stewardship, with measurable goals.","Identify one ethical or practical obstacle (e.g. cost, convenience, behaviour change) and how you would overcome it.","Present your proposal in a 4-minute pitch to a ‘panel’ (the class), using Word Bank vocabulary."], rubric:[["1–2","Issue vague; no evidence or measurable goals."],["3–4","Issue identified with limited evidence; proposal general."],["5–6","Clear issue supported by evidence; realistic proposal with measurable goals; obstacle addressed."],["7–8","Well-evidenced, practical and measurable proposal; obstacle analysed thoughtfully; strong, explicit link to Zayed's stewardship and intergenerational justice."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Stewardship or Exploitation?", prompt:"Sort each action: does it reflect environmental stewardship, or exploitation of the environment?", bins:["Stewardship","Exploitation"], items:[["Planting trees to green areas of desert",0],["Breeding endangered Arabian oryx in a protected reserve",0],["Overhunting wildlife until a species becomes endangered",1],["Regulating falconry to protect wild bird populations",0],["Clearing mangroves without considering the ecosystem",1],["Dumping waste into the sea to save money",1],["Planting mangroves to protect the coastline",0],["Wasting water in a desert country with scarce supplies",1]]},
  quiz:[["What does 'khalifah' mean in Islamic teaching on the environment?",["Humans as custodians or trustees of the Earth","A type of tree","A desert animal"],0],["Which island did Sheikh Zayed develop as a wildlife reserve?",["Saadiyat Island","Sir Bani Yas Island","Yas Island"],1],["What is 'afforestation'?",["Cutting down forests","Planting trees in an area where there was previously no forest","A type of farming"],1],["Which endangered species is associated with conservation efforts on Sir Bani Yas?",["The Arabian oryx","The polar bear","The giant panda"],0],["What does 'intergenerational justice' mean?",["Justice only for older people","Laws about schools","Present generations' duty to protect the interests of future generations"],2],["Why are mangroves environmentally important?",["They protect coastlines, store carbon and provide habitats","They have no environmental value","They are only decorative"],0],["Which global climate conference did the UAE host in 2023?",["COP21","COP26","COP28"],2],["What does Zayed's approach suggest about development and the environment?",["Development must always stop to protect nature","Development can proceed while treating the environment as a trust","The environment does not matter in a desert"],1]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain why environmental stewardship is a moral responsibility, not only a practical choice.",
  copy:{facts:["Sheikh Zayed championed environmental stewardship through desert afforestation, the Sir Bani Yas Island wildlife reserve, the protection of the Arabian oryx and responsible falconry.","The Islamic concept of khalifah describes humans as custodians of the Earth, and intergenerational justice means we must protect the environment for future generations.","Zayed's approach shows that development and conservation can be balanced; the UAE continues this through mangrove planting and hosting COP28 in 2023."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we complete Unit 1 by exploring how to live Zayed's legacy today."
 },
 {
  id:"l6", lesson:6, title:"Living Zayed's Legacy Today", unit:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach", art:"future",
  dna:"Unit 1 vocabulary check! Define each term in your own words: Legitimacy, Coexistence, Sadaqah, Shura, Stewardship. This is our final lesson of Unit 1 — which of Zayed's values do you think is most needed in the world today?",
  dnaQs:["Legitimacy and Coexistence — what does each mean?","Sadaqah and Shura — what does each mean?","Stewardship — what does it mean? Which of Zayed's values is most needed today?"],
  dnaHint:"Legitimacy: the acceptance that a leader's authority is rightful. Coexistence: living together peacefully with mutual respect despite differences. Sadaqah: voluntary charitable giving in Islam. Shura: the principle of consultation in decision-making. Stewardship: responsible care of something entrusted to us, such as the environment. For the final question, any value can earn credit if you justify your choice with a current example.",
  intentions:["Synthesise the core values of Zayed's moral approach studied across Unit 1.","Analyse how these values can guide personal choices and civic responsibility for young people in the UAE today.","Evaluate what it means to inherit a legacy, and design a personal commitment to living these values."],
  criteria:["I can synthesise Zayed's core values with accurate examples from across the unit.","I can analyse how these values apply to real choices facing young people today.","I can evaluate the meaning of legacy and justify a personal commitment."],
  concept:{key:"Identity", related:"Legacy, Citizenship, Responsibility", soi:"A moral legacy endures when each generation interprets its values and translates them into action in new circumstances.", context:"Identities and Relationships — exploring how inherited values shape personal and national identity.", uae:"Draws together the unit: values-based leadership (Lesson 1), tolerance (Lesson 2), generosity and Zayed Humanitarian Day (Lesson 3), shura and unity (Lesson 4) and stewardship (Lesson 5); links to the UAE's ‘Year of Zayed’ (2018), CAS and the DP Extended Essay."},
  words:[["Legacy","The values, achievements and influence that a person leaves behind for future generations."],["Civic responsibility","The duties of a citizen or resident to contribute positively to their community and society."],["Synthesis","Combining different ideas to form a coherent whole or a new understanding."],["Moral exemplar","A person whose conduct serves as a model of moral values for others."],["Active citizenship","Participating in the life of a community through service, involvement and responsible action."],["Commitment","A dedication or pledge to a value, cause or course of action."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion A — Knowing and Understanding", steps:["Choose ONE current UAE initiative, organisation or programme that you think continues Zayed's legacy (e.g. a volunteering platform, a humanitarian campaign, an environmental programme, a tolerance initiative).","Research it using at least two reliable sources.","Record: what it does, who it involves, and which of Zayed's values it reflects (from Lessons 1–5).","Identify ONE way young people of your age can take part in it, or a similar initiative.","Evaluate: how faithfully does it reflect Zayed's values, and where might it go further?"], prompts:["The initiative I chose and my two sources","What it does and who it involves","Which of Zayed's values it reflects","One way young people can take part","How faithfully it reflects Zayed's values, and where it could go further"], success:"Strong answers make a precise link to at least one value studied in the unit (e.g. a volunteering programme reflects generosity and active citizenship; a mangrove-planting project reflects stewardship). Answers should be accurate and verifiable rather than general praise. For the top band, provide a thoughtful evaluation — for instance, whether an initiative reaches all communities or whether participation is genuine rather than symbolic — and identify a realistic way for students to contribute, such as through CAS."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"Reflect on Unit 1: Lesson 1 (values-based leadership), Lesson 2 (tolerance and coexistence), Lesson 3 (generosity and humanitarian work), Lesson 4 (justice, shura and unity) and Lesson 5 (environmental stewardship).", steps:["Write a synthesising essay paragraph responding to the question: ‘Is a legacy something we inherit, or something we must choose to continue?’","Draw connections between at least THREE lessons from the unit and use at least FIVE key terms from across the unit's Word Banks accurately.","Conclude with a reflection on what living Zayed's legacy could mean in your own life over the next two years."], model:"A legacy is both inherited and chosen: we inherit its values, but it survives only if each generation actively decides to continue it. Sheikh Zayed left a clear moral legacy — values-based leadership grounded in humility, a vision of coexistence among people of many nationalities, generosity expressed through humanitarian work, unity built through shura and compromise, and stewardship of the environment as a trust for future generations. Yet these values are not preserved automatically by monuments or anniversaries; they endure only when people translate them into action in new circumstances, such as using digital platforms to coordinate volunteering or applying stewardship to the challenge of climate change. For me, living this legacy over the next two years could mean committing to active citizenship through CAS, consulting others fairly in group work and making environmentally responsible choices, because a legacy that is admired but not practised soon becomes only history.", rubric:[["1–2","Little synthesis; few connections between lessons; minimal key vocabulary."],["3–4","Some connections, mostly descriptive; 2–3 key terms used."],["5–6","Clear synthesis across three lessons; 4 key terms used accurately; personal reflection included."],["7–8","Sophisticated synthesis across the unit; 5+ key terms used precisely; argument on legacy is nuanced; personal reflection is specific and convincing."]], starters:["A legacy is both inherited and chosen, because ___.","Sheikh Zayed's moral legacy includes ___, ___ and ___.","These values endure only when ___.","For me, living this legacy over the next two years could mean ___, because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task (Unit Showcase)", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Legacy in Action Plan", steps:["Individually or in pairs, choose ONE of Zayed's values studied this unit that you want to put into action.","Design a realistic ‘Legacy in Action’ plan for the next school term (it could become a CAS project), with clear, measurable goals.","Identify the community it would serve, the resources needed and one ethical consideration (e.g. respecting the dignity of those you help, consulting stakeholders).","Present your plan to the class as a 3-minute ‘legacy pitch’, explaining how it connects to Zayed's moral approach.","As a class, compile all plans into a shared ‘Legacy Wall’ and agree on ONE plan to pursue together as a year group."], rubric:[["1–2","Plan is vague; little connection to Zayed's values."],["3–4","Basic plan with general goals; limited connection to the unit."],["5–6","Clear, realistic plan with measurable goals; ethical consideration addressed; explicit link to a value from the unit."],["7–8","Practical, ambitious and well-justified plan; ethical considerations analysed thoughtfully; insightful connections across Zayed's moral approach."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Which Value of Zayed's Legacy?", prompt:"Sort each modern action into the value from Zayed's moral approach it best reflects.", bins:["Tolerance & coexistence","Generosity & giving","Shura & unity","Stewardship"], items:[["Organising an interfaith community iftar",0],["Volunteering at a food distribution campaign during Ramadan",1],["Consulting all members of a group before making a decision",2],["Joining a mangrove-planting day",3],["Welcoming a new student from a different country",0],["Donating to a reputable relief appeal on Zayed Humanitarian Day",1],["Reducing single-use plastics in the school canteen",3],["Helping two rival groups compromise to reach an agreement",2]]},
  quiz:[["What is a 'legacy'?",["The values and influence a person leaves for future generations","A type of building","A government department"],0],["Which value is reflected in Sheikh Zayed's majlis tradition?",["Isolation from the people","Closeness to people and consultation","Competition between citizens"],1],["Which initiative reflects Zayed's environmental stewardship?",["The Sir Bani Yas Island wildlife reserve","The Federal National Council","The Ministry of Tolerance"],0],["What does 'active citizenship' mean?",["Avoiding involvement in community life","Only voting in elections","Participating in community life through service and responsible action"],2],["Zayed Humanitarian Day is linked to which value?",["Generosity and humanitarian giving","Military strength","Economic competition"],0],["The ‘Year of Zayed’ (2018) marked…",["The founding of the UAE","The centenary of Sheikh Zayed's birth","The discovery of oil"],1],["Why might a legacy be ‘chosen’ rather than simply ‘inherited’?",["Because values are automatically preserved by monuments","Because legacies are only about history","Because values endure only when each generation acts on them"],2],["Which is the strongest example of living Zayed's legacy as a student?",["Memorising facts about him without changing behaviour","Committing to a CAS project that serves the community and respects others","Talking about values but not acting on them"],1]],
  exit:"Write ONE specific action you will take this term to live one of Zayed's values, and explain which value it reflects.",
  copy:{facts:["Zayed's moral approach combines values-based leadership, tolerance and coexistence, generosity, justice and shura, and environmental stewardship.","A legacy endures only when each generation actively interprets its values and puts them into action in new circumstances.","Young people can live Zayed's legacy through active citizenship, volunteering, fair consultation and environmentally responsible choices — including through CAS."]},
  next:"Unit 1: Zayed's Moral Approach is complete! Next, we begin Unit 2."
 }
];
